                <h2>Lead with the Conclusion: The Root Cause of Cold Launch Failures is a Shallow Creative Library</h2>
<p>Many operators pin their hopes on "one viral creative" during product launches, only to find their campaigns stall after two days of spending. TikTok's algorithm needs sufficient creative signals to calibrate its audience model, and a handful of creatives simply cannot provide this data foundation.</p>
<p><strong>The core logic is straightforward: use the breadth of your creative library to accelerate algorithm learning, and use testing density to improve the odds of finding a hit.</strong></p>
<hr />
<h2>Strategy 1: Build Your Creative Library First, Don't Wing It</h2>
<h3>Optimal Creative Mix</h3>
<p>A healthy cold launch creative library should contain these four content types:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Type</th>
<th>Proportion</th>
<th>Purpose</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Core product USP showcase</td>
<td>30%</td>
<td>Strengthen category awareness</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Use case / comparison content</td>
<td>40%</td>
<td>Spark purchase desire</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>UGC-style / authentic content</td>
<td>20%</td>
<td>Build trust</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Promo / limited-time hooks</td>
<td>10%</td>
<td>Drive immediate conversions</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>A home goods seller entering the Southeast Asian market prepared 28 creatives in advance, launching them in batches. During the first two weeks, they focused not on ROI but on CPM and CTR metrics. By identifying 8 creatives with stable delivery, they kept cold launch CPA within 85% of their target.</p>
<h3>Quick Production SOP</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Break down core product selling points</strong>: List video-worthy angles from three dimensions—functionality, price, and differentiation</li>
<li><strong>Match content templates</strong>: Product reviews, unboxings, before/after comparisons, pain point solutions</li>
<li><strong>Batch production</strong>: Create 3-5 versions of the same selling point (different openings, durations, subtitle styles)</li>
<li><strong>Label systematically</strong>: Naming convention should include "category_USP_version_date" for easy data attribution</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Strategy 2: Test in "Sets" Not "Singles" to Identify High-Potential Creatives Faster</h2>
<h3>Tiered Testing Allocation</h3>
<p>Don't dump 20 creatives into one ad set. Instead, allocate testing budgets as follows:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Round 1 (Days 1-3)</strong>: 50-100 RMB per creative, running A/B tests in parallel to collect CTR and CPM data</li>
<li><strong>Round 2 (Days 4-7)</strong>: Promote the top 30% CTR creatives, increase budget to 200-300 RMB per creative, test conversion rates</li>
<li><strong>Round 3 (Days 8-14)</strong>: Keep creatives with ROAS above 1.5 for scaled spending; creatives below threshold are immediately paused</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Operational insight</strong>: A utility app team sets their benchmark at—creatives with CPM exceeding 150% of target AND CTR below 1% are immediately marked as "underperformers" and excluded from Round 2 testing, saving wasted spend.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>Key Metric Priority During Cold Launch</h3>
<p>A common beginner mistake: overemphasizing ROAS during cold launch. Instead, focus on these metrics:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>CTR (Click-through rate)</strong>: Determines if the creative can capture user interest</li>
<li><strong>CPM (Cost per mille)</strong>: Indicates audience targeting relevance</li>
<li><strong>Video completion rate</strong>: Shows if the first three seconds have sufficient hooks</li>
<li><strong>Add-to-cart rate</strong>: Reflects landing page effectiveness</li>
</ol>
<p>ROAS weight should be deprioritized until after the testing phase, as small sample sizes during cold launch make ROAS highly volatile and unreliable.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Strategy 3: Iterate Creatives Proactively—Don't Let Top Performers Go Unprotected</h2>
<p>When a creative starts delivering consistently, many operators fall into a "don't touch it" mentality, fearing data drops from adjustments. But even top performers have lifecycles.</p>
<h3>Creative Decay Warning Signs</h3>
<ul>
<li>Slower spend velocity under the same budget</li>
<li>CTR declining more than 15% day-over-day</li>
<li>Rising CPM</li>
</ul>
<p>When these appear, the creative is entering its decay phase. Take immediate action:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Quickly replicate similar scripts</strong>: Keep core selling points, swap actors/scenes/BGM</li>
<li><strong>Add fresh creatives to pipeline</strong>: Prevent gaps when主力 creatives go offline</li>
<li><strong>Reduce budget concentration</strong>: Distribute spending across 3-5 different creatives</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Final Takeaway</h2>
<p>TikTok Shop cold launch is essentially a "creative consumption battle." The depth of your creative library determines how long you can sustain, how many potential winners you can surface, and whether you have ammunition for scaling. Stable cold launch accounts don't necessarily have more brilliant ideas—they have more systematic creative management.</p>
<p><strong>Before any cold launch, every operator should ask three questions: Is my creative library at least 20 deep? Do I have clear testing group structures in place? Do I have decay warning mechanisms set up?</strong></p>
<p>If the answer to all three is "not yet," spend another week building your creative library before launching. The patience pays off.</p>
